  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nI'll implement a novel method based on compressed sensing principles with gradient projection and multi-resolution optimization. The core idea is to represent the optimal step function as a sparse linear combination of basis functions while maintaining the necessary properties for autocorrelation density analysis. This approach should achieve a lower global minimum compared to the previous 1.507902 result.\n\nThe strategy will use:\n- Floating optimization of coefficients with gradient-like updating\n- Successive upward vector projection to maintain non-negativity\n- Auto-convolution density analysis via FFT to locate critical constraints\n- Multi-stage gradient evolution with exponential smoothing of constraints\n\nThe key innovation is expressing the sequence as a product of orthogonal basis vectors, which reduces the effective dimensionality of the optimization problem and allows us to bypass some of the value constriants while still finding global minima. This should lead to several orders of magnitude better autocorrelation properties.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nWe formulate the optimization as:\nVariables: `a` sequence of n real coefficients (n up to 2048)\nObjective: minimize `(2n * max(convolve(a,a))) / (sum(a)^2)`\nsubject to:\n- `sum(a) > 0.01`\n- `0 <= a_i <= 1000` for all i\n\nWe'll use a simplex projection and L1-penalized gradient descent. The constraint handling and numerical stability will be achieved by iterative clipping and then normalization to unit sum (after increasing to meet minimal sum requirement).\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Support up to 2048-dimensional vectors if time permits\n2. Initialize using:\n   - Uniform distribution (low dimension first)\n   - Gaussian pulse initialization scaled up to meet sum constraints\n   - Bilinear initialization preserving peak values\n   - Reflection-based initialization to create mirrored patterns\n\n3. Add time-based scheduling for simplex parameter evolution:\n   - Start with 1-iteration per optimization cycle, exponentially increase to 3-5 cycles\n   - Monitor movement through simplex space\n\n4. Use FFT-based convolution evaluation every 5 iterations to maintain proper constraint tracking\n\n5. Maintain sparse representations by periodic re-normalization to extreme proportional values\n\n6. Switch to density optimization approach if primary method stalls\n\n7. Set termination criteria: criterion improved by less than 1e-5 for 30 consecutive history iterations\n\n8. Ensure non-negativity via projection after each step\n\n9. Bypass coefficient bounds during intermediate steps to speed up optimization\n\n10. Integrate exponential smoothing into gradient updates to resolve maxima/minima positions accurately\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n- Primary search space: 256-512 dimensions (float and parent support)\n- Initial oscillation phase (50 cycles) with learning rate 0.05\n- Secondary phase (400 cycles) with momentum 0.9 and learning rate 0.01\n- Tertiary phase (if time remains) with direct gradient algorithm and constraints relaxation\n\nThe candidate optimization will balance dimension selection against time constraints:\n1. Start with smallest dimension first due to evaluation cost\n2. Run 10 cycles initially to stabilize the coefficients\n3. Check for smooth maxima in convolution using numpy's medianfilter\n4. Implement time discounting: `(remaining_time/total_time)^(cycle_number) * initial_learning_rate`\n\nBudget and Robustness:\nWe assume standard library calls (numpy, scipy) are competitive in terms of wall-clock time for medium-sized vectors. The dominant cost will be FFT convolution of sequences of size n where `n <= 1500`.\n\nTime allocation:\n- 800s for primary optimization cycles\n- 600s for secondary grid optimization\n- 100s for final touch-up and validation\n\nValidation returns best_candidate_sequence with:\n- Explicit storage every 5 iterations\n- Early termination if evaluation cost with direct method becomes prohibitively high\n- Maintains finite evaluations (each iteration must be checked)\n\nReturns the final candidate sequence as a list of floats in order, first coefficients will be dense in the beginning and sparse later stages.\n\nExample output: `[0.1, 0.5, 0.2, 0.7, 0.3, ...]`",
